Write the **controller** for **Foray**, a territorial maze-raiding contest between
two ant colonies. You are not building an application — you are writing the
*intelligence* that drives one colony's three agents, compiled to WebAssembly and
pitted head-to-head against an opponent controller. Once compiled, your controller
runs on its own with no model in the loop, so all of the strategy has to live in
the code you ship.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. A complete starter is already in place
there; build your controller on top of it.

## How it works

Foray is a capture-the-flag-style game on a mirror-symmetric maze. Each agent is a
**soldier** (defender) on its own half and a **raider** (forager) on the enemy
half — its role flips at the border. Raiders eat the enemy's seed caches and bank
them by carrying them home; soldiers tag enemy raiders, sending them back to their
nest and dropping their load. Two rules make Foray its own game: a raider's speed
**degrades with its load** (carry weight), and eating **royal jelly** grants the
eater tag-immunity (not the inverse). Read the specs for the exact rules — your
controller has to reason about both twists to win.

Your controller is invoked **once per tick** with your colony's observation (the
`world`) and must return the moves for your three agents (the `action`). The
observation and the action are the only channel to the game; you never see or
touch authoritative state.

