## Your starting point

The starter workspace at `{{workspace}}` is a self-contained Cargo project:

- `controller/src/lib.rs` — the controller you fill in. It builds and runs as-is,
  but it is the floor (a trivial legal move per agent); replace it. You write a
  single `fn decide(world: &World) -> Action` and the `controller!` macro wires it
  to the wasm ABI — see `specs/contract.md`.
- `vendor/foray-core` and `vendor/foray-controller-sdk` — the game engine (the
  `World`/`Action` contract types, identical to what the harness scores you
  against) and the SDK (the ABI plumbing plus maze helpers: a passability grid, a
  BFS, and a legal-action builder). Path dependencies of your controller; do not
  edit them.
- `references/` — the three baseline controllers, each as readable Rust source and
  a pre-built `.wasm`. Worked examples of reading the `world` and returning an
  `action`; opponents to dismantle, not templates to copy.
- `maps/mirror-32x16.toml` — the shipped board.
- `tools/` — a separate workspace for the local `foray` CLI, so you can run matches
  against the baselines exactly as the harness will. Build and use it as
  `specs/baselines.md` describes.

## How to build

Your controller compiles to a `wasm32-unknown-unknown` **core module**:

```bash
cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p controller
```

This emits `target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/controller.wasm` — the exact
artifact the harness loads and scores. Keep the crate named `controller` with
crate-type `cdylib` so that path is stable. The wasm target is already installed.

## What to deliver

A controller that **beats the baselines comfortably** — decisively beating
`border-soldier`, the canonical scoring opponent, on the shipped map — and that
plays the actual game: it balances offence and defence across its three agents tick
by tick, times each bank against carry weight rather than over-loading, and spends
royal jelly to run a heavy load home through a defended border. A controller ported
straight from a published capture-the-flag strategy will over-load and ignore
jelly's inversion, and lose; the work of this case is to play *Foray*, not generic
CTF.

Iterate locally: build the `foray` CLI, run your controller against
`references/border-soldier.wasm` (and the other two, and a few seeds), watch the
result and the replay, and refine until you win clearly. Stay inside the per-tick
fuel and memory limits in `specs/sandbox.md` — a controller that traps, runs out of
fuel, or returns an invalid action forfeits. When `cargo build --release --target
wasm32-unknown-unknown -p controller` produces a `controller.wasm` that beats the
baselines, you are done; the module is your submission.
